{"id": "c0408545-0159-4e57-9a8c-04ca83b7c2bd", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T16:14:50.712445", "source": "environment", "parent_id": "27355c30-935c-4e47-91ba-6a061df02521", "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-be12ff776a29a940", "observation": {"content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"phase\": \"exploration\", \"command\": \"pip list 2>/dev/null | grep -iE \\\"torch|megatron|deepspeed|pipe|bubble|simulat\\\" ; echo \\\"exit=$?\\\"\", \"exit_code\": 0, \"timed_out\": false, \"elapsed_seconds\": 0.416, \"output\": \"exit=1\\n\", \"output_truncated\": false}"}], "is_error": false, "kind": "BenchmarkObservation"}, "action_id": "8bf1c9ee-097b-4ba4-b091-502d18ff3a48", "extended_content": [], "kind": "ObservationEvent"}
{"id": "0d193e00-4e3d-4a01-8c41-0806e1c08dfd", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T16:16:25.351373", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "c0408545-0159-4e57-9a8c-04ca83b7c2bd", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": "Confirmed: the masked repository does not exist in the environment. The environment only has task.json, with a note saying \"Design-only task. Repository and computation are not required.\" No GPU, no PyTorch, network disabled.\n\nTherefore, I have to base the design on the known open-source implementation of \"Zero Bubble\" pipeline parallelism. This question refers to 1F1B, 1F1B-I, ZB-1p, ZB-2p — these are the scheduling strategies from the Zero Bubble Pipeline Parallelism paper (Qi et al., 2024, \"Zero Bubble (Almost) Pipeline Parallelism\"), and the reference repository is the ForgeAI/zero-bubble-scheduler repo. That repo contains the following:\n- `sim.sh` / simulator: `sim/` includes the schedule validator and profiler.\n- Scheduling algorithms: 1F1B, 1F1B interleaved (1F1B-I), ZB-V, ZB-1P, ZB-2P under `schedlib/` (e.g., `schedule.py`), with functions like `get_1f1b_schedule`, `get_1f1b_interleaved_schedule`, `get_zbv_schedule`, `get_zb1p_schedule`, `get_zb2p_schedule`.\n- Profiler: cost model `sim/prof.py` (estimator) and `sim/sim.py` event-driven simulator that computes makespan. Throughput is measured as step time / makespan. Number of microbatches m =_mb, number of stages _pp, number of chunks per stage, etc.\n- Benchmark script `bench.py` runs the simulator over configurations and outputs best makespan per schedule type.\n\nKnown repo structure (ForgeAI/zero-bubble-scheduler):\n- Files: `sim.py`, `sim.sh`, `prof.py`, `sched.py`, `schedule.py`, `bench.py`, `validate.py`(?), `graph.py` (Gantt chart plotting), `model.py`(?). Let me recall: the README lists `sim.py` (simulator), `sim.sh`, `bench.py` (benchmark script), `prof.py` (profiler/estimator), `sched.py` (scheduling algorithms), `schedule.py` (schedule data structures), `graph.py` (visualization), `validate.py`? Also, is there a Megatron integration in the `megatron` folder? Actually there is a separate branch/fork for Megatron-LM integration (\"Zero Bubble Plugin\").\n\nMain APIs (from memory): functions in `sched.py`: `oneF1B(_pp, _mb, ...)`, `oneF1BInterleaved`, `zbV`, `zb1p`, `zb2p`. `sim.py` usage: `python3 sim.py -pp 8 -mb 48 --type zb2p` etc. Also `-c` number of chunks, `-i` interleaving, `--chained`. Profiler `prof.py` estimates cost. `bench.py` sweeps configurations and outputs makespan to `bench/`. In this simulator, cost is measured in units of time (per-layer cost model, f/b/w time), and throughput is inversely proportional to step makespan.\n\nSince I can't actually verify the repository (it's masked and doesn't exist in the environment), I have to be explicit: the repository contents are assumed to be the public ForgeAI/zero-bubble-scheduler implementation, and I should state this as an assumption. The plan should be robust: if an actual repo is present, use the simulator's schedule validator + timing simulator, and if not, use analytical schedule-length formulas.\n\nThe task says to limit this turn to inspection findings and a proposed approach, with stage=plan handoff.\n\nAlso, the task mentions the question budget is 6 and researcher_topics is empty. Alright, let me draft the plan.\n\nKey domain facts (from the Zero Bubble paper, Qi et al. ICLR 2024):\n- ZB-1p: achieves a bubble rate lower than 1F1B. Like 1F1B, bubble overhead decreases as m grows. Requires m to be at least some minimum (m ≥ p) and constraint p ≡ 1 mod 3? Actually ZB-1p requires m to be a multiple of… hmm. In the paper: ZB-1P requires m being a multiple of p? Let me recall precisely.\n\nPaper: \"Zero Bubble (Almost) Pipeline Parallelism\" — algorithms ZB-V, ZB-1P, ZB-2P.\n- 1F1B bubble rate: (p-1)/(m+p-1)… actually the 1F1B bubble time = (p-1)(f+w+b)/(m(f+w+b))? For m microbatches, step time = (m+p-1)(f+w+b); bubble ratio = (p-1)/(m+p-1). So throughput increases with m, saturating as 