rk should be a named deliverable item.\\n\\n4. Feasibility handling: Recording, not skipping, infeasible (method, m) pairs is correct. Additionally, the design must specify what \\\"improves as m increases\\\" means when a method's curve is undefined at some m (e.g., monotonicity assessed only over the feasible domain, with the feasible-domain caveat reported per method).\\n\\n5. Deliverable at result stage: full configuration matrix, exact sweep grid (contiguous m = 1..4p primary plus extended powers-of-two grid to m = 128), metric definitions (makespan, normalized throughput = ideal-work/makespan), feasibility rules, the Q1 monotonicity criterion (non-decreasing over feasible domain, strictly increasing over a substantial range, saturation behavior, Spearman ρ plus marginal-gain statistic), the Q2 dual criterion from item 2, validity-check list (validator on every schedule, determinism repeats, 1F1B closed-form sanity check, recompute-cost accounting), and a checklist mapping each output artifact to its sub-question. Include a feasibility section covering expected runtime/cost of the simulator sweep (it should be trivially cheap) and what could fail.\\n\\nDo not claim any experiment was run; the output is a design with rationale, controls, analysis plan, and feasibility.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null, "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "critic_result": null, "kind": "MessageEvent"}
