ne Parallelism reference structure (schedule generators for 1F1B/1F1B-I/ZB-1p/ZB-2p, deterministic event-driven simulator with f/b/w cost model, validator, bench sweep). Feasibility probe run during design: evaluating the full grid arithmetic (304 closed-form throughput evaluations, dedup of contiguous 1..4p plus extended grids) completed in 0.0001 s in-process, and the closed-form sanity check matched exactly (1F1B p=8, m=128 → N = 0.9481481… = 1 − 7/135), demonstrating trivial sweep compute cost and validating the metric definition and the (p−1)/(m+p−1) cross-check formula used in V3. Analytical anchors: 1F1B bubble ratio (p−1)/(m+p−1); interleaving divides bubble by v; ZB-2p attains its near-zero-bubble schedule class without requiring large m (Qi et al., ICLR 2024). Researcher-approved refinements incorporated: dual Q2 criteria reported separately; equal-v comparison with ZB-2p recompute charging as named deliverable V4; feasible-domain monotonicity rules; p fixed at {4,8}; feasibility/runtime section included.", "assumptions": ["The target repository is a faithful instantiation of the Zero-Bubble reference implementation (schedule generators + deterministic simulator + validator + bench sweep); none of its code is present in the masked workspace, and the design does not depend on version-specific internals beyond documented knobs.", "Cost-model conventions: CM1 f=b=w=1 is convention-free; CM2 (b=2f) adopts the repo's own w-handling verbatim, applied uniformly to all methods including ZB-2p recompute work.", "'High throughput' for ZB-2p is defined operationally by the researcher-approved criteria (a) 0.90× of N(128) at m ≤ p and (b) ≥ the best of the other methods' N(128), reported independently.", "Feasibility constraints on m for ZB-1p/ZB-2p are exactly those enforced by the repository's own generator/validator; the design records rather than assumes them.", "Determinism: the event-driven simulator has no stochastic components, so repeat runs must be bit-identical.", "Scope boundary (fixed, per researcher): simulator/analytical makespan is the only measurement basis; real GPU execution, memory-capacity checks, communication beyond the repo cost model, and failure/recovery are out of scope."], "uncertainties": ["Which specific m values the repo's ZB-1p/ZB-2p generators reject (divisibility/minimum-m rules) — resolved at run time and handled by feasible-domain reporting, not by assumption.", "Whether a given repo build's simulator charges inter-stage communication separately; if absent, conclusions are limited to compute-bubble effects (failure mode F3).", "Exact numeric constants of the interleaved-1F1B and ZB-2p closed-form bubble formulas in the paper's notation; V3 cross-checks pin these to the repo simulator semantics before criteria are applied.", "No experiment has been executed and no outcomes are claimed; all numbers in this handoff come solely from the closed-form arithmetic feasibility probe, not from method schedules."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-946370d46b03784e", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-946370d46b03784e", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"stage\": \"result\", \"summary\": \"Submit complete design-only experiment for pipeline throughput sweep\", \"answer\": \"EXPERIMENT DESIGN (design-only; no experiment has been run)\\n\\nObjective\\nAnswer: (Q1) Does normalized throughput for 1F1B, 1F1B-I, and ZB-1p improve as microbatch count m increases? (Q2) Does ZB-2p maintain high throughput even at small m? Measurement basis is simulator/analytical makespan only; real GPU execution is out of scope by design (scope boundary, not a limitation).\\n\\n1. System under study (design basis)\\nThe published Zero-Bubble pipeline-parallelism reference implementation structure: schedule generators for 1F1B, 1F1B-interleaved (1F1B-I), ZB-1p, ZB-2p; a deterministic event-driven timing simulator producing per-device timelines and step makespan from a cost model; a schedule validator; and a bench-style sweep harness. No code exists in the masked workspace, so the design is specified against this structure and remains valid for any faithful instantiation (repo simulator primary; closed-form schedules as cross-check/fallback).\\n\\n2. Configuration matrix (fixed controls; p is NOT swept)\\n- Methods: 1F1B (v=1), 1F1B-I (v=2), ZB-1p (v=1), ZB-2p (v=2).\\n- Pipeline stages p \\u2208 {4, 8} \\u2014 two separate fixed experiments.\\n- Interleaving chunks v = 2 for all interleaved methods. Equal-v comparison of 1F1B-I vs ZB-2p isolates the effect of input-interleaved immediate-forward recomputation from plain chunked interleaving.\\n- Cost models (both reported): CM1 unit-cost f=b=w=1 (primary); CM2 b=2f with w treated per repo convention (robustness). Same cost model applied identically to all methods; ZB-2p's extra forward-recompute ops are charged as real forward work in the makespan (validity check V4).\\n- Communication: only what the repo cost model includes; no additional network modeling.\\n\\n3. Sweep grid (independent variable m)\\n- Primary: contiguous m = 1..4p (m=1..16 for p=4; m=1..32 for p=8) \\u2014 dense coverage of the small-m regime central to Q2.\\n- Extended: powers-of-two-style grid {1,2,4,8,16,32,48,64,96,128} merged with the contiguous grid; m=128 is the \\\"large-m asymptote\\\" reference point for criterion (a).\\n- Total cells: 2 p-values \\u00d7 2 cost models \\u00d7 4 methods \\u00d7 \\u2264 ~48 m-values \\u2248 768 makespan evaluations (deduped).\\n\\n4. Metrics (dependent variables)\\n- Makespan T(method, m, p, v, CM): simulated single training-step time, in the repo's abstract time units.\\n- Ideal work per step W_id = m\\u00b7(f + b) (per microbatch required non-recompute compute, per-unit-device; w handled per CM convention, identical across methods). Normalized throughput N = W_id / T \\u2208 (0,1], equal to 1 \\u2212 bubble ratio; primary comparison metric, comparable across m.\\n- Raw throughput R = m / T (microbatches per time unit) reported as secondary.\\n- Bubble ratio B = 1 \\u2212 N reported for figure parity with the paper.\\n- m90: smallest m with N(m) \\u2265 0.90\\u00b7N(128) (efficiency-at-low-m statistic).\\n\\n5. Feasibility rules (undefined points)\\n- Run the repo's schedule generator/validator for every (method, m); any rejection (e.g., divisibility or minimum-m constraints for ZB-1p/ZB-2p in a given version) is recorded in a per-method feasible-domain table D_method, never silently skipped.\\n- Monotonicity is assessed only over each method's feasible domain; the feasible-domain caveat is reported next to every Q1 verdict (e.g., \\\"1F1B improves over all m\\u22651; ZB-1p improves over its feasible m \\u2208 D_ZB1p\\\").\\n\\n6. Analysis plan and decision criteria\\nQ1 (per method \\u2208 {1F1B, 1F1B-I, ZB-1p}, per p, per CM, over feasible domain):\\n- (i) Non-decreasing: N(m_{i+1}) \\u2265 N(m_i) \\u2212 \\u03b5 for all consecutive feasible points, \\u03b5 = 1e-9 (deterministic simulator).\\n- (ii) Substantial strict increase: N(m_max) \\u2212 N(min feasible m) \\u2265 0.05 absolute and strictly increasing across \\u2265 50% of consecutive feasible pairs.\\n- (iii) Saturation: |N(128) \\u2212 N(64)| \\u2264 0.02 (curve flattens toward the zero-bubble bound).\\n- (iv) Spearman \\u03c1(m, N) \\u2265 +0.95 over feasible domain.\\n- (v) Marginal-gain statistic: \\u0394 = N(4p) \\u2212 N(p), reported per method/p (expected: ZB-1p starts higher than 1F1B at m=p; similar \\u0394 at large m).\\nVerdict \\\"improves as m increases\\\" iff (i)\\u2013(iv) hold; (v) is descriptive.\\n\\nQ2 (ZB-2p), criteria reported separately, not folded:\\n- (a) Self-referential: N_ZB2p(m) \\u2265 0.90\\u00b7N_ZB2p(128) for all feasible m \\u2264 p.\\n- (b) Competitive ceiling: N_ZB2p(m) \\u2265 max{N_1F1B(128), N_1F1B-I(128), N_ZB1p(128)} for all feasible m \\u2264 p.\\nReport a per-(p)\\u00d7(CM) pass/fail table for (a) and (b) independently; (b) is the stronger claim. Secondary: ZB-2p m90 vs the other methods' m90.\\n\\n7. Validity checks (named deliverables)\\n- V1 Validator-on-every-schedule: 100% of accepted schedules pass the repo validator; rejects logged.\\n- V2 Determinism: each cell run twice; makespans must be bit-identical (event-driven simulator, no stochastic components).\\n- V3 Closed-form cross-checks (required): simulated 1F1B bubble ratio within 1e-6 of (p\\u22121)/(m+p\\u22121); 1F1B-I bubble ratio matches the interleaved closed form scaled by v; ZB-2p simulated bubbles fall in the paper's near-zero, m-independent/rapidly-decaying bubble class (near-flat high N even for m \\u2264 p).\\n- V4 Recompute-cost accounting (named deliverable): artifact demonstrating that every ZB-2p input-forward recompute op appears as a forward-duration interval on the device timeline inside the makespan \\u2014 verified by counting recompute ops in the generated schedule and matching the simulated busy-time increment; ensures the equal-v ZB-2p vs 1F1B-I comparison charges ZB-2p's extra work.\\n\\n8. Output artifacts \\u2192 sub-question checklist\\n- A1: N-vs-m curve panel (per p, per CM, all 4 methods) \\u2192 Q1 and Q2.\\n- A2: Q1 verdict table (per method \\u00d7 p \\u00d7 CM: checks i\\u2013iv, \\u03c1, \\u0394, feasible domain) \\u2192 Q1.\\n- A3: Q2 pass/fail table for criteria (a) and (b) separately (per p \\u00d7 CM), plus m90 table \\u2192 Q2.\\n- A4: Feasible-domain / infeasibility table D_method \\u2192 caveat reporting for Q1 and Q2.\\n- A5: Validity report V1\\u2013V4 \\u2192 internal validity of all claims.\\n- A6: Raw-throughput R and bubble-ratio B appendix \\u2192 figure parity with the paper.\\n\\n9. Feasibility section (cost and failure modes)\\n- Runtime: makespans come from closed-form schedules or a discrete-event simulator over \\u2264 4\\u00d7128\\u00d72 operator slots per device; the entire \\u2264768-cell sweep is seconds of CPU. A closed-form arithmetic probe of the full grid executed during design (304 evaluations) took 0.0001 s, confirming trivial compute cost; a single core suffices.\\n- What could fail: (F1) ZB-1p/ZB-2p generators reject some m \\u2014 handled by \\u00a75 feasible-domain rules; report which m and which constraint fired. (F2) A repo variant's w-convention differs from CM2 \\u2014 handled by applying the repo's own convention uniformly and documenting it; CM1 is convention-free. (F3) Simulator lacks explicit comm model \\u2014 comm is then out of scope and stated so; conclusions limited to compute-bubble effects. (F4) If the exact repo build is unavailable, run the design on closed-form schedule constructions plus the paper's bubble-ratio formulas; V3 becomes the primary evidence path. None threaten answerability of Q1/Q2, only caveat granularity.\\n- Scope boundary (fixed): no real execution, no GPU, no memory-capacity feasibility analysis, no failure/recovery behavior; ZB-2p memory-neutrality is an analytical property outside this experiment.\", \"evidence\": \"Repository inspection (this session): /workspace contains only task.json (\\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required\\\"); filesystem-wide searches found no 1F1B/interleaved/zero-bubble code, no torch/megatron/deepspeed/simulator packages; Python 3.14.4; GPU and network disabled \\u2014 hence design-only against the published Zero-Bubble (Almost) Pipeline Parallelism reference structure (schedule generators for 1F1B/1F1B-I/ZB-1p/ZB-2p, deterministic event-driven simulator with f/b/w cost model, validator, bench sweep). Feasibility probe run during design: evaluating the full grid arithmetic (304 closed-form throughput evaluations, dedup of contiguous 1..4p plus extended grids) completed in 0.0001 s in-process, and the closed-form sanity check matched exactly (1F1B p=8, m=128 \\u2192 N = 0.9481481\\u2026 = 1 \\u2212 7/135), demonstrating trivial sweep compute cost and validating the metric definition and the (p\\u22121)/(m+p\\u22121) cross-check formula used in V3. Analytical anchors: 1F1B bubble ratio (p\\u22121)/(m+p\\u22121); interleaving divides bubble by v; ZB-2p attains its near-zero-bubble schedule class without requiring large m (Qi et al., ICLR 2024). Researcher-approved refinements incorporated: dual Q2 criteria reported separately; equal-v comparison with ZB-2p recompute charging as named deliverable V4; feasible-domain monotonicity rules; p fixed at {4,8}; feasibility/runtime section included.\", \"assumptions\": [\"The target repository is a faithful instantiation of the Zero-Bubble reference implementation (schedule generators + deterministic simulator + validator + bench sweep); none of its code is present in the masked workspace, and the design does not depend on version-specific internals beyond documented knobs.\", \"Cost-model conventions: CM1 f=b=w=1 is convention-free; CM2 (b=2f) adopts the repo's own w-handling verbatim, applied uniformly to all methods including ZB-2p recompute work.\", \"'High throughput' for ZB-2p is defined operationally by the researcher-approved criteria (a) 0.90\\u00d7 of N(128) at m \\u2264 p and (b) \\u2265 the best of the other methods' N(128), reported independently.\", \"Feasibility constraints on m for ZB-1p/ZB-2p are exactly those enforced by the repository's own generator/validator; the design records rather than assumes them.\", \"Determinism: the event-driven simulator has no stochastic components, so repeat runs must be bit-identical.\", \"Scope boundary (fixed, per researcher): simulator/analytical makespan is the only measurement basis; real GPU execution, memory-capacity checks, communication beyond the repo cost model, and failure/recovery are out of scope.\"], \"uncertainties\": [\"Which specific m values the repo's ZB-1p/ZB-2p generators reject (divisibility/minimum-m rules) \\u2014 resolved at run time and handled by feasible-domain reporting, not by assumption.\", \"Whether a given repo build's simulator charges inter-stage communication separately; if absent, conclusions are limited to compute-bubble effects (failure mode F3).\", \"Exact numeric constants of the interleaved-1F1B and ZB-2p closed-form bubble formulas in the paper's notation; V3 cross-checks pin these to the repo simulator semantics before criteria are applied.\", \"No experiment has been executed and no outcomes are claimed; all numbers in this handoff come solely from the closed-form arithmetic feasibility probe, not from method schedules.\"]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-6161760d-5824-4a62-80c9-d1734bd2477b", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "Submit complete design-only experiment for pipeline throughput sweep", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
